WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.740
So, like I said, my name
is Tanner Linsley.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.840
I am from Utah

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.710
and I love JavaScript so much.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.110
I also love React even more.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.990
I'm happy to be able to talk about
React at a JavaScript conference.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.720
I'm on GitHub probably
way too much,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.900
writing way too many
open-source libraries.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.000
I'm also on Twitter and YouTube,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
so you can come say
hi to me there if you want.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.830
I know that you're
all probably feeling like...

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.430
you can't see my screen.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:41.430
Yeah.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.540
You're probably feeling
a little bit like my son here,

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:51.080
just really tired and wanting to
consume that last little bit of JavaScript.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.080
Just do your best.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.720
I know it's hard but we're going
to make it through it together.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.700
Because today I'm talking
about React hooks

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.800
and more specifically
custom React hooks

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.000
and I'm so excited about this

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.000
and I hope you are too.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.000
I know it's been a noisy year.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.000
You guys probably feel like...

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.510
whether you love React or not
you probably feel this way.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.150
So, I'm going to do my best today

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.340
to make sure that this is not
just another React hooks tutorial.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.340
It's going to be fast

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.340
because I have a lot of
content to go over

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.340
and there's going to be a lot of code.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.640
Hopefully it's a lot of fun.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.560
I'm going to be saying “use” a lot.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.590
So, it's hard, it's a tongue twister.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.190
You'll see.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.730
So, before we get into
the bulk of my talk,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.100
let's go through React Hooks
Basics really quickly.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.100
Classes are out.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.400
We no longer write with classes.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.000
Everything is just a function now
and that is so nice.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.280
It makes us so that components
that used to look like this to use State

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.890
don't have to look like that anymore,

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.890
we can just use useState.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.890
It's so much better in my opinion

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.400
or we can use useReducer.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.950
Class Lifecycles are
no longer a thing.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.750
I'm so glad to be done
with these things.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.630
Also, we don't have to do manual
change detection anymore

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.230
with the old class lifecycles.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.460
We can just use the useEffect
function from React,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.940
pass it some dependencies

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.940
and everything will just
synchronize for us.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.600
It also comes built-in with memoization
and computed state

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.670
and things around memoizing
values and functions.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.870
Where we could do
this with React before

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.800
but now it's built in.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.500
So, we can make sure that we're
only computing or doing work

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.600
when we need to be doing it.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.850
And last, the most important thing
that I'd like to talk about today is

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:39.750
Custom Hooks,

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.490
which a custom hook is really just a
function that runs inside of a component

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.540
that can run other hooks
or other functions

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.140
like useState, useReducer, whatever.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.450
And they can be recursive,
they can go forever.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.550
You can have a custom hook
call another custom hook

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.550
call another custom hook
call another custom hook

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.850
and everything will just come back in

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:58.950
and it just works.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:02:59.800
It's amazing.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.800
It makes patterns like this

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.500
that before we had to
nest all these callbacks

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.500
and create higher-order components
and render props

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.500
and functions as children,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.000
all that gets to go away

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.750
and we're simply left with
a couple of function calls

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.690
that we can make inside
of our components.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.260
Custom hooks are so powerful.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.500
I believe that they give us
a couple more affordances

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.050
that we didn't have when we
were using class components.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.500
I think that they encourage us
to build our own solutions

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.800
because it makes building
our own solutions easier.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.800
The API is more expressive.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.580
I also believe that building hooks,
custom hooks

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.880
is way more portable and shareable

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.250
than building custom components

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.450
and trying to move logic
around with components.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.560
The other great thing is that custom
hooks can be component aware

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.600
and abstract essentially anything
that we want them to.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.400
This makes them very powerful

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:55.350
for integrating with essentially
anything we want to integrate

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.970
in the outside world
away from React.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.970
And last but not least, I think

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.440
that they highly encourage
rapid iteration

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.840
for some of the reasons that
I'm going to talk about today.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:10.650
So, the biggest reason that I believe
that all those things are true

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.150
is that hooks,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.250
that with the introduction
of custom hooks,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.150
I think that we're going to see
or have already seen

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:21.690
a return to components
handling user interface

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.100
and hooks handling business logic.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.150
And if this seems like a no-brainer,
you're ahead of the curve

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.530
and if it doesn't, that's what I'd
like to talk about today.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.470
Through so many migrations of old
projects to hooks going 100% hooks

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.350
and new greenfield projects

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.800
where I was able to experiment with
new patterns around custom hooks

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.950
and see how far can we push
the limits of this new API,

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.780
I've discovered a couple of use cases

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:52.950
that have really sparked new interest
for me with this hooks API.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.890
Most of them around
building portable UI utilities,

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.000
a few of them,
well actually a lot of them,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.660
around managing global state
and server state

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.270
and also encapsulating business
logic for our applications

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.000
inside of these custom hooks.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.900
And I want to share with you a
couple of these use cases today

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.700
to show you how custom hooks

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.950
can really change the way that you
think about writing your applications

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.950
and to be more productive.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.720
Why not start off with something
that is so hot right now,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.000
you can't ship an app without it

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.400
and it sometimes is the first
thing that we all think about,

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.700
probably the most important
thing we all think about,

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:34.250
that's Dark Mode.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.100
You can't ship without it, right?

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.200
This is so hot right now.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.400
Everybody wants a dark mode.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.370
Everybody's got that toggle
at the top of their site,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:45.770
you go between light and dark mode.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.100
It's pretty easy to do.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.450
You can just set up some
state and toggle between it

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.220
with a function set is
dark to true or false.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.320
It's really that easy.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:59.220
I wanted something a little more
sophisticated for my dark mode.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.690
I don't know if you guys have
ever seen matchMedia,

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:11.030
but it's an API that you can use to match
CSS media queries in JavaScript

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.830
and detect certain things
about the users environment.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.030
Right here we have a matchMedia

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.410
that's detecting whether the user
prefers a light or a dark theme

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.240
and we can actually set up
our state to start with that,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.310
so that if the user has a dark or light
theme preference on their device,

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.710
our state now starts
with that preference

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.000
and they can toggle between it.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.990
But who has this enabled
on their computer?

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:33.590
This auto mode.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:34.890
I do.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.460
And I love it

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.880
and I thought wouldn't it be
amazing if we could make it

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.100
so our light and dark themes

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.830
would actually react to that
automatic mode on our devices.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:46.530
Turns out it's not too hard.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.800
We can take an effect
and put our matchMedia inside it

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.900
and listen to changes
to that matchMedia

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.350
and update our dark variable
whenever that changes.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.280
And if we put all that together
inside of our app right here,

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:00.720
it's all just handled for us now.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.720
The Sun sets,
we go into dark mode.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.120
The Sun rises,
we go into light mode.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.120
It's actually pretty fun to play with.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:08.400
But the problem
I see here is that

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.480
a lot of this logic is now taking up
space in my app component

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.800
and I don't really like that

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.000
talking about “clean code”
opinions here,

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:21.300
but this I see is a great example of where
we can make our first custom hook.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.900
We can actually just take all that logic
and rip it out into its own file,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.300
we'll call it useDarkMode

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.180
and everything in here
is exactly the same

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:31.910
except for what we're returning
is dark at the very end

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.550
and that makes this useDarkMode
hook extremely versatile.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.400
In our app now we can import
that file call useDarkMode

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:41.000
and it's going to give us true or false,

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.800
but it's not just true or false,

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:44.800
it's true or false
that changes over time

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.490
and all of the lifecycle
and updating

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.030
and all of the intelligence
of that dark mode

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:50.530
is built into that hook

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.000
and all we have to do is call it

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.070
and that is our first
custom hook for today.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.670
That's when everybody claps.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:04.050
So, the next thing that I thought
every application needs,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.050
everybody has written
this a thousand times,

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.750
clicking outside of an element

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.900
and I don't know why the browsers
don't make it easier to do this natively,

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:14.760
but I feel like I'm implementing
this every day.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.830
Clicking outside of a menu
or a modal or whatever, right,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.230
and then you want to
do something to it.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:24.550
I thought, there's not a better excuse
to write a custom component for this

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:27.540
and I thought we might as well
just make the assumption

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.260
that this is going to be a custom
hook right out of the gate.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.280
So, I assumed that I could take
a ref created by useRef,

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.810
put it on to whatever element
I want to keep track of

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.310
and then have a handle
or something like console logging,

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:43.810
hey, you clicked outside of this thing

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.600
and I could pass those two things
to this useClickOutside hook

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:48.800
and it would just work

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.250
and I wrote this actually before
I wrote the useClickOutside hook

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:56.300
to see how reliable it was
to design APIs around hooks

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:57.300
before even writing them.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.500
It turns out it wasn't too difficult.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.200
We start with the function signature,

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.200
we throw in an effect

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.910
that's going to set up our
event listeners on the document

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:08.510
for when we click on the document.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.710
Those call our internal listener here

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:11.450
that checks to see

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.480
if the target we clicked is inside
of the element that we're tracking,

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.050
and if it isn't, it calls our callback.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.050
Turns out it just works

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.460
and we would probably
use this for a little bit

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.990
and think Wow! This is great,
I'm a genius,

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:29.000
but eventually you might
notice something very strange.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.500
There's something fishy
going on here

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.700
and it has to do with
the useEffect dependencies.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.500
Now if you're using
the eslint-plugin-react-hooks,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:38.340
which you should be,

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.110
99% of the time it does exactly
what you want it to,

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.810
it auto fills Effect dependencies
for your hooks.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.600
So helpful, I love it.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:47.800
You can just hit Save

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:51.550
and if you have eslint setup
it will just do the changes for you.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.100
And that's what it did here
when we built our hook.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:54.700
Here on the right,

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.750
you can see it automatically
filled in the callback

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.690
in the elRef down there
in our dependencies

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:00.960
but the problem is that

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.530
the callback is changing
every single render

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.630
from where we're using it on
the left side here onClickOutside.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.030
We're creating it every single time

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:11.040
which means that those
event listeners are firing off.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.140
Every single time that we render

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.340
we're adding and removing
event listeners.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:16.540
That is not cool.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.750
Well, it turns out it's pretty easy to just
wrap that in a useCallback from React

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:22.800
and it's going to make it

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.350
so that that callback function doesn't
change unless the dependencies change.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.000
There are no dependencies yet.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.600
So, nothing's going to change

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.230
and it will stop creating those event
listeners over and over and over,

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.630
but this begs the question for me,

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:39.080
why do I have to have that
useCallback around that function?

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.140
This probably in advance educates,

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:46.670
but I feel like there's nothing inside
of my useClickoutside hook

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.540
that cares about this
callback changing

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:52.500
and I decided that
that's part of the API.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.250
So, I wanted to get rid of it

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.250
and one of the easiest
ways to do that

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.420
is to instead of referencing
the callback directly,

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.720
we can use this pattern

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:05.490
of tracking the latest version
of that callback in a ref itself,

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:07.090
and when we do that

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:10.100
the callback ref is now added
to the dependency array

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.100
which will never change

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:14.300
but the value that
we hold inside it will

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.710
and that way we're only setting up
and tearing down that event listener

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.170
when we actually need to

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.740
when the element that we're
tracking actually changes.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.510
So, now there's no callback,

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:28.380
we can pass whatever function we
want into that useClickoutside hook

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:32.520
and that is our second
custom hook for today.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.100
What's fantastic about this is you
can move this around your app,

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.600
you can import it to as many
components as you want

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.900
or you can just copy and paste
this into another app.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.200
There's no external dependencies
other than React

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:49.200
and I do this all the time.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:50.370
Greenfield project,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.000
I'm like oh! I really love that hook,

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.540
I go back to the last project
that I was working on,

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.580
I open the file, I copy it
and I move it over,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:57.380
it's that easy.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.480
I don't even really rely on external
libraries that do a lot of this stuff

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:02.680
because it's just so simple.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:10.060
Something that isn't simple is state,
particularly global state.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:14.150
Global state is highly opinionated.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.450
There's a new way to
manage global state.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:18.650
Every day it comes out
there's a new library.

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:20.250
It's kind of noisy.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.850
And we're going to be talking
a lot about global state here

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:25.050
for the last part of this talk.

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.980
Global state in my opinion is something
that's always trying to 1up you.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:33.080
It's because you think
you have a handle on it

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.280
and then your requirements change

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.380
and everything changes in your app

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.900
and it kind of pulls the
rug out from underneath you.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.600
This is usually how
global state starts

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.460
and you're like
Wow! This is so simple.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.800
I have a hook, it's going to return
some global state theoretically

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.130
and then for some reason it can
quickly turn into something like this,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:55.340
not to name names or anything,

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:57.540
but this is something
I would try and avoid.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:01.710
I don't want to have to go
to these links to use global state.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.410
So, I'm going to take you
on a little bit of a journey

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.410
of finding global state today

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:10.420
and it's going to be so introspective
and enlightening

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:14.390
that maybe you'll want to actually
handle your own global state after today.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.020
Let's start by creating a global store.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:21.960
We can use React context to
pass values down our React tree,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.660
not have to pass them through props.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:28.400
We can start with a store provider
that's just a wrapper around context

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:34.950
that will set up some state with
a store and a setStore function right there

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.350
that we can pass down
through our context value

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:43.220
and then we'll have a useStore custom
hook that just wraps around that use context

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:44.720
and what that does

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.860
is now inside of our app
we can declare some initial state,

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.450
pass that initial state
into our store provider

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:54.400
and now that state is going to be
available to all the components inside of it,

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.400
including the little Todos
component down there.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.500
And inside of our components

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:03.500
we can consume the store pretty
easily by just calling useStore

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.250
which gives us the
current state of the store

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:07.300
and a way to update it

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:08.800
which right now is just setState.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:11.800
setState is kind of boring though

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:13.800
and it's prone to break

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.800
and I don't really feel good

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.390
about manipulating the store
directly from a component.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.290
So, what if we took it
a step further to useReducer.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:27.790
useReducer is really pretty
simple to use in my opinion.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:29.580
Just create a reducer function here.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:30.620
We can do it in our app.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:34.540
We can pass that reducer function
into our store provider we created

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:37.250
and instead of useState
now we can just useReducer,

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.000
pass the reducer through the initial state

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:42.100
and instead of a setState function
to directly modify the store

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.600
now we have a dispatcher we can use.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.600
So, in our components

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.550
instead of importing the setState,
we get the dispatch

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.550
and now we can dispatch actions

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:52.300
and have a little bit more confidence

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:55.520
about the actions that are
taking place inside of our app,

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:00.560
especially even for components
like an individual to do

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:02.300
where we're not necessarily
reading from the store

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.500
but just dispatching,

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:06.550
we can still import the store

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:10.000
and get a hold of dispatcher
to send off some events.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.000
This pattern is really simple.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.500
It will probably get you pretty far,

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.600
but at the end of the day it might bring
up questions about unnecessary renders.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:20.720
You know, I'm rerendering every
component in my app,

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.720
every single time that
the state changes

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.020
even if I'm only dispatching.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:28.420
Well, it turns out we can use
a concept called multiple contexts

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.520
to aid this a little bit.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.700
A Multi-Context Global Store

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:36.300
was first introduced to me
by my friend Kent C. Dodds

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:40.400
and he's made it popular
through a couple of blog posts

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:41.600
which are just fantastic

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:43.950
and it describes creating
two separate contexts

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.470
for your store and your dispatcher

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:48.670
and sending them both down the line

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:52.000
and creating another
useDispatch custom hook there.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:55.080
So, now inside of our components
if we want the store,

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:56.580
we use the useStore hook,

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.490
and if we want the dispatcher,
we use the useDispatch hook.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:03.220
This makes it so that in components
that are only using the dispatcher

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.860
they're not going to get updated
every single time the store updates

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:12.070
and that will get rid of a lot of weird
unnecessary renders around that scenario.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.270
Let's take it a step further.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.570
What about a single store?

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:20.100
It is probably not going
to scale for very long

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.080
if you have a lot of global
state in your application,

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.480
you don't want every single
component updating

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:28.950
when this state that you're consuming
updates that global state.

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.850
So, we can take it a step
further with multiple stores.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.790
We can take all of that logic that
we had to create our global store

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:37.790
and just wrap it into a function

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.500
and we can create as many of
these global stores as we want

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.000
by passing them in
a reducer in initial state.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.200
So, if we wanted to do a store,

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.200
we just pass it the todosReducer,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.640
it gives us back the provider
and a couple of hooks to consume it

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:53.200
and we're off to the races.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.200
We come into our app,

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.380
we provide our Todos
to the entire app

00:16:57.680 --> 00:16:59.500
and I want some global state

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.100
to manage all of my menus
being open and stuff like that.

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.920
So, I'll make a layout store as well

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:07.920
and I'll wrap that around my app

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.250
and then inside of our component

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:14.030
like our Menu that we wanted to click
outside and have it change around,

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:17.060
we can hook it up to
that useLayoutStore

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.570
by both subscribing to
the store to see if it's open

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:21.620
and the dispatcher

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:24.410
to make sure that we can toggle
it close when we click outside of it.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:29.900
The next step in our
journey is persistence.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:32.680
Now our global store isn't
really persisting anything.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:34.580
This is extremely important

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:36.980
because your users are
going to reload the page

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:38.480
and everything's going to disappear.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:40.580
So, let's throw in some local storage.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.630
Local storage is pretty easy to use
especially if you're using a reducer.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:52.200
So, right here we can instantiate our
reducer initial value from local storage,

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:54.200
and also every time a reducer runs,

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.940
we can save the resulting state
into local storage using a key

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.240
and now every single time
that your users reload the page

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.540
they're going to get the last state
that they were left with.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.000
I know this is where you guys want to be

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:11.400
and I'm working hard
to get you there.

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:15.590
Just to make sure
that everybody is awake.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:17.090
Like that!

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.090
I got some t-shirts.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:27.290
All right.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:32.000
Moving on.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.100
What about Remote Data Persistence?

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.980
So, local storage is great

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:44.280
but eventually users are going to
want some remote data persistence,

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.450
they're going to want to
store their data on a server,

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:48.920
they're going to want things to
sync between their devices.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:50.420
They're so entitled.

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:54.420
So, let's talk about Server State.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.420
Now I say server state because

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.420
I believe that it is truly
different from global state

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:01.420
but it is the same in spirit.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.840
Server state introduces
some interesting challenges

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:09.780
around moving from synchronous APIs
to asynchronous APIs.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.980
We're no longer interacting
with a store or a dispatcher,

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:16.850
we are interacting with fetching assets

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:21.090
and sending mutations
or queries to the server

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.090
and expecting them to do things,

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.350
and along with that
we're going to notice

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:28.900
that a lot of our components
where we'd previously wired up

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:30.900
things like dispatch and the store

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.200
that kind of becomes a little bit
of a question mark now,

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.200
where are we getting those things?

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:39.500
Well, turns out that our components

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:45.480
are really great places to compose
business logic and user interface together

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:50.200
and this can be so easy
that sometimes it's a detriment.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.100
We take things like local state markup,

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:53.700
UI events, styles,

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:56.500
things that I feel are meant
to be in components

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.450
and we kind of mix them
together with all this state

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:02.030
and these external utilities

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:03.700
or maybe expensive computation

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.270
or side effects that are
happening on servers,

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.270
and we take all that and we smoosh
it together inside of our components

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:14.340
and sometimes it can make
them hard to reason about

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.740
and I'd like to propose
a new pattern today

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.910
where we need to remember that
custom hooks are free now.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.250
There's no cost of adding
a new custom hook

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:24.900
other than the cost of abstraction.

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:28.900
You can ask Kent C. Dodds how he feels
about the cost of abstraction,

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:30.000
he talks about it a lot.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:35.930
But today, I'd like to introduce a new
layer of abstraction to our journey,

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:38.230
they're just custom hooks.

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:40.730
There's really nothing
to say other than that

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.500
and they're going to proxy
all of our business logic

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:47.710
between our components
and the external services

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:50.240
and the external features that
we want to integrate with them.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.450
So, in this case, we're going
to take our Todos component,

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.350
we're going to write
a useTodos custom hook

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:00.250
that's going to do all the heavy lifting
for us between our data.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:01.750
What would that look like?

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:03.800
We could just start with a useTodo,

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:06.330
we can import useTodos
into our component

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.330
and just expect that
it returns Todos

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:10.000
and that would be enough for now.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:13.850
This useTodos hook could
do whatever we wanted to

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:15.400
as long as it returns Todos,

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:16.800
that's all we care about.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:19.450
The implementation details
are hidden from the component,

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:20.700
it doesn't care and it shouldn't.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:24.450
So, if we wanted to implement
our existing in-memory store,

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:28.550
we could just import our store
and return the Todos from it

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:30.000
and we would be fine,

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:32.800
but remember when you talk
about asynchronous state

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.400
and state coming from the server,

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.000
that changes a lot of things,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:38.150
especially if we're not
talking about suspense

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.650
which is a whole other talk,

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.650
but let's assume that we're
not using suspense

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:44.450
and this is what something
like that would look like.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.450
We know that it's going to
be loading at some point,

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:47.800
we know there could be an error

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.200
or we know we could have Todos.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:53.300
And as long as we conform
to this function signature,

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.610
we can actually do a lot
with this custom hook,

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.450
this useTodos custom hook
that we just made.

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:03.700
We could use an effect
and like a tool like axios

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:05.200
to communicate with a ref server

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.750
that would load our Todos for us

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:11.700
and we can manage all of the loading
state and everything ourselves

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:16.060
or we could use a very common
hook called a usePromise

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:18.700
that will let us pass a function
that returns a promise

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:21.800
and handle all of that isLoading
and error state for us.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.900
Again, as long as we're
returning the Todos,

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:26.100
the loading state and the error,

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:29.340
any component that's consuming
Todos isn't really going to care

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:32.110
and we could do the same thing,

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:36.350
take this pattern and apply the same
pattern to anything doing a mutation.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:40.390
So, if we had a todo component that
was going to toggle something,

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:43.890
we could have that
useToggle todo custom hook

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.390
take care of that for us.

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:51.350
So, instead of importing
some REST API or axios

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:52.800
directly inside of our component,

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:54.900
we import useToggleTodo,

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:57.400
we call it and it'll give us a function,

00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:58.350
and you know what? 

00:22:58.350 --> 00:22:59.750
Instead of just giving us function,

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.300
let's assume it's going
to be asynchronous,

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:04.680
it'll give us a function and the loading
state and error state as well

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:08.820
and again that unlocks us the ability

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.520
to do whatever we want in
that useToggleTodo hook.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.600
We can do whatever we want
as long as we're returning a function

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:16.200
that the component can call

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:18.200
and an isLoading and error state.

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:20.930
So, we could use our
in-memory store again

00:23:20.930 --> 00:23:23.600
and just hook it right up
to our Todo store,

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.030
isLoading and error is false and null
all the time if we're doing this

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:30.600
or we can go into the REST paradigm

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:31.650
and just use axios

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:38.250
to issue off some type of a post command
to our server to toggle this Todo,

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:45.800
but this can get pretty
confusing very quickly

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:48.000
if you're managing all these side effects,

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:51.430
especially when we start thinking
about things like caching.

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:55.400
What happens when we're using these
custom hooks multiple times in our app

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:57.400
if we need Todos in multiple places,

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.000
are they separate copies?

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.470
Are we reduplicating things?

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.310
Are we creating race conditions?

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:06.000
And if we're caching,
when are we refetching that data?

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:09.610
There are so many questions
that happen along there.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:11.900
Well, we don't have to stop there.

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:14.510
I used Todos custom hook

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:18.050
and it doesn't have to communicate
directly with our server,

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.050
it could use other hooks as well.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:22.500
There's a library created
called React query

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:25.560
that helps with scenarios like this.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.760
You can use React query to give
it a function that returns a promise,

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:33.000
it'll handle caching and invalidation

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:38.350
and make sure that you're not duplicating
Todos across your entire app

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:40.000
even if you use it multiple times.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:43.580
I mean you'll notice that
the really cool part is that

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:46.200
our return signature
is exactly the same.

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:50.980
So, we just moved from this weird funky
promise based way of fetching our data

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:52.880
to something like React query

00:24:53.100 --> 00:25:00.030
and all of our components that use the
useTodos hook didn't have to change at all.

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.030
It's just an implementation detail.

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:05.050
Same thing we could
do for the mutations.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:07.870
We could add in React query
for the useToggleTodo

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:15.600
and in fact React query handles
a lot of the invalidation for mutations.

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:17.600
So, running this mutation would

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.400
invalidate all of the other queries
on the page getting Todos

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:23.400
and it would cause all
of them to update.

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:27.590
It's not very unsimilar
to tools like Apollo.

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:31.300
And I don't use GraphQL a whole lot,

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.430
but when I do, I do enjoy using Apollo.

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:37.900
And what would it take to
switch to something like Apollo?

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:42.200
Well, we could import Apollo
and make a query,

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:44.350
use the useQuery hook from Apollo

00:25:44.650 --> 00:25:47.940
and again we're just returning
Todos as loading an error,

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:51.000
we just moved from
React query to Apollo,

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.510
we didn't have to change
anything in our entire app.

00:25:54.910 --> 00:25:57.580
So, same thing with
causing a mutation,

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.860
as long as we're returning that ToggleTodo
and isLoading error,

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:03.860
again our mutations don't have to change.

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:07.400
So, we were able to just
in the last five minutes,

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:11.100
well, go from in-memory
management of our data

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.300
to promises to React query and Apollo,

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:15.300
we can probably even move
to Relay if we wanted,

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:17.300
all because we're using

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:21.810
this pattern of creating
your own custom hooks

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:25.250
to abstract our own business
logic in our apps

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:29.450
away from our components
to make them smarter

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.650
and to make them more reliable.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:34.450
This pattern to me
is extremely powerful.

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:37.750
I see basically all the time
in my applications,

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.790
all my components are communicating
with custom hooks

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:42.790
to handle most of their business logic

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:46.200
which are then reaching out to
possibly even more custom hooks

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.000
or external APIs or whatever.

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:53.340
This creates a really nice encapsulation
around what is user interface,

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:54.840
what is business logic

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:58.410
and what are the services I'm
trying to use in my applications.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.300
Thinking from this perspective

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:07.000
you can start to imagine so
many great custom hooks

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:09.750
that you would want to
use inside of your apps,

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:14.000
but maybe not something that would
be shareable outside of your app.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.200
It's okay if a custom hook is proprietary
to something you're working on

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:19.700
if it makes you more productive.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:25.110
So, these custom hooks and the idea
of custom hooks is so powerful,

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:28.310
I take things to the extreme

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:32.950
and I create tons of open-source
libraries built on custom hooks.

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:35.720
Those four are just a few of them.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:39.000
React table is in fact just a hook,

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.490
it actually doesn't
render anything for you,

00:27:41.990 --> 00:27:45.360
it just is a hook that gives you everything
you need to render your own table,

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:47.360
it's a really interesting concept,

00:27:47.560 --> 00:27:50.700
but my hope and invitation
to all of you today

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:53.900
is to look at your hooks in your apps

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:57.440
and think of ways that you can
make yourself more agile,

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:00.610
abstract your business logic
into your custom hooks

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:04.210
and make your components dumber
thereby making them smarter.

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:07.800
So, be sure to check me out at
GitHub, Twitter, YouTube.

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:09.580
Come talk to me.
I'd love to chat.

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:12.790
And can't forget nozzle.io,

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:16.620
that's my company that
I started back in Utah.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.220
We build marketing software

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:22.730
that helps agencies know
where they rank in Google.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:23.730
So, thanks.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:28.400
[Applause]

